BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:"Hunting for the Snark Illustrations" with the Jack London Society
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261106T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261106T123000
UID:2610196KjA6m9JOE-HAjitHFB_zA
DESCRIPTION:Step inside the House of Happy Walls Museum for a fascinating 
 exploration of Jack London’s photographic journey aboard the Snark with 
 Professor Emerita Any Tucker.\n\nJack London always referred to The Cruise
  of the Snark \\(1911\\) as an “illustrated book.” The pictures were c
 entral to his authorial vision of the voyage to the South Pacific that he 
 and Charmian London embarked on in April of 1907. Maddeningly\, in the pro
 cess of preparing the manuscript of The Cruise of the Snark for publicatio
 n\, Macmillan & Co. made a hash of the author’s design\, misplacing some
  of the book’s 119 photographs and scrambling the captions Jack so caref
 ully assembled.\n\nTucker explains\, “While Jay Williams\, my co-editor 
 for Volume 13 of Oxford UP’s ‘Complete Works of Jack London\,’ has b
 een collating various iterations of the essays collected in ‘The Cruise 
 of the Snark’ to prepare a definitive copy text\, I’ve been trying to 
 track down the original photographs for the book.  The treasure hunt has 
 proved more challenging—and surprising—than expected. The pictures\, m
 ost by Jack with a few by Martin Johnson\, the Snark’s young cook and en
 gineer\, are scattered among far-flung sources—beginning with Charmian L
 ondon’s huge collection of photo albums at the Huntington Library and mu
 ltiple binders of prints and negatives housed in the California State Park
 s collection at Sonoma Barracks. My search eventually took me to archives 
 in Chanute\, Kansas\; Auckland\, NZ\; and the British Museum\, for it turn
 s out that there was another\, unacknowledged contributor to Jack’s illu
 strated book.”\n\nAbout the speaker: Amy Tucker is Professor Emerita of 
 the English Department of Queens College\, CUNY\, where she taught America
 n literature and cultural studies. She has published essays on art\, trave
 l and literature\, and served as guest editor of a volume on Charmian Lond
 on for “Women’s Studies: An Inter-Disciplinary Journal.”  Her books
  include “The Illustration of the Master: Henry James and the Magazine R
 evolution\;” “Visual Literacy: Writing about Art\;” and\, most recen
 tly\, “I’m Used to Playing Lead: Memoirs by Women in Jazz\,” to be b
 rought out by Equinox in January\, 2027. Tucker and Williams have received
  a two-year grant from the National Endowment for the Humanities to work o
 n their volume for “The Complete Works of Jack London.”\n\nThis event 
 is part of a speaker series co-hosted by Jack London Park Partners and the
  Jack London Society\, honoring the 150th anniversary of Jack London’s b
 irth. JLPP is grateful for the support of the Jack London Society and its 
 scholars in bringing this speaker series to life. Join us at Jack London S
 tate Historic Park throughout 2026 for a compelling lineup of speakers cel
 ebrating Jack London’s legacy.  Learn more at https://jacklondonsociety
 .org/.
LOCATION:Jack London State Historic Park
URL:https://jacklondonpark.com/events/jl-speaker-series-amy-tucker/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
